   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="2">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Percentage of HIV-positive women who received ART during pregnancy and/or labour and delivery</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The indicator measures the effectiveness of ART programme which includes quality of care as an early warning indicator for development of HIVDR. Numerator: Number
of people living with HIV and on ART who have a suppressed viral load (&lt;1000 copies/mL). Denominator: Number of people on ART who had a VL measurement in the
past 12 months. Targets: Aligned with the Revised National ZNASP M&amp;E Plan (2021 - 2025) : 92% for 2021, 93% for 2022, and 94% for 2023. Actual N/D will be
provided when reporting. Baseline: national LIMS Report (2019) 85% (528,902 / 622,422), all facilities country-wide. Currently the SR (MoHCC) is piloting an
interoperability solution between LIMS and eHR, in which case eHR feeds into DHIS2. Source for Reporting: Laboratory programme data from the Laboratory Information
Management System (LIMS) that will feed into the DHIS2. Data Disaggregation: During reporting the recommended level of data disaggregation will be (TBC): 1.Gender
(male, female) 2.Age (0&#x2013;4, 5&#x2013;9, 10&#x2013;14, 15&#x2013;19, 20&#x2013;24, 25&#x2013;29, 30&#x2013;34, 35&#x2013;39, 40&#x2013;44, 45&#x2013;49, 50+) 3. Pregnant women and infants. Note: (i) This indicator will be reported
reflective of the national picture on VL suppression across sub-populations in line with joint HIV Strategy and ZNSP IV (2021-2025). (ii) Ministry of Health and Child Care
is currently able to report by sub-populations and there are strategies to improve VL testing across all subpopulations. These include a) adequate budgetary provision in
the NFM3 to support promotional materials in terms of quarterly newspaper articles/adverts/editorials for 3 years, b) working closely with civil societies on patient
education through treatment literacy sessions in communities, c) provision in the 2021-2023 GF funding cycles to support treatment literacy activities in the communities
in 20 non-PEPFAR supported districts (PEPFAR is supporting the remaining districts). (iii) Male Engagement activities , collaborating with ZNNP+ on a VL awareness
campaign with support from ASLM, community based DSDs, rolling out patient electronic record system, color stickers in the NFM3 grant to help to quickly identify
patients who are due for VL and those with high viral loads, having POC samba and gene Xpert, specifically for infants, adolescents and pregnant women. Currently
(2020) all the 14 conventional viral load labs are connected to LIMS. Patient samples from health facilities (clinic name, District and Province) are submitted to the testing
laboratory accompanied by a lab request form which identifies the patient by Name and ART number. The information on the request form is captured in LIMS and test is
performed. At the end of the month a report of all the results that were reported during the month is made and the total number of samples with suppressed viral load
results is determined. Viral load suppression may be measured using three different data sources. In the Zimbabwean context the source for reporting on this indicator
will be: Laboratory programme data from the Laboratory Information Management System (LIMS) that will feed into the DHIS</narrative>
    </description>
